From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] net: stmmac: retry a failed RX refill from a timer

stmmac_rx_refill() allocates with GFP_ATOMIC | __GFP_NOWARN and stops at the
first page it cannot get. Nothing retries it except the next RX poll. Once the
DMA owns no descriptor it raises no RX interrupt, so that poll never comes: the
queue stays stalled after memory is back, and only ndo_open refills the ring.
On a MAC with hardware flow control the full MTL RX queue then keeps the link
partner paused for as long as the stall lasts.

This is our own fix, not a backport. Upstream has none (read 2026-09-27: not in
6.18.54, torvalds master v7.3-rc4, or netdev's net-next test branch
net-next-2026-09-27--06-00). The design is the one the netdev review asked for:

  - "[PATCH net v4 2/2] net: stmmac: Prevent indefinite RX stall on buffer
    exhaustion" (Sam Edwards, patchwork netdevbpf 14506044, changes requested)
    returned the budget from stmmac_rx() while stmmac_rx_dirty() >=
    STMMAC_RX_FILL_BATCH, so NAPI polled on.
  - Jakub Kicinski on v3 (<20260331195927.3353cc6d@kernel.org>): "If the system
    is OOMing having ksoftirq busy looping indefinitely is not going to be very
    helpful. 1) only react if the fill level is below some critical threshold,
    2) try to add some delay (timer)? before the retry". On v4
    (<20260402080542.293e8729@kernel.org>): "I meant we need both a threshold,
    and a delay". And (<20260402174049.0ac088cc@kernel.org>): "say we arm a
    timer and let the IRQ be unmasked. The timer just runs napi_schedule().
    napi_schedule() is thread-safe, if IRQ fires with the timer armed - no
    problem."
  - The first half of that series went in as 0bb05e6adfa9 ("net: stmmac:
    Prevent NULL deref when RX memory exhausted", v7.1, in 6.18.53). Its text
    keeps the stall open: "the driver simply *hopes* that the next
    stmmac_rx_refill() succeeds, risking an indefinite stall of the receive
    process if not." Its author's note on v6: the timer "as requested by
    Jakub" is still to come.

So: v4's threshold, plus the timer the review asked for instead of the busy
loop.

What the patch does:

  - struct stmmac_channel gets rx_refill_timer, set up with the channel's
    rx_napi in stmmac_napi_add(). The callback only calls napi_schedule().
  - stmmac_napi_poll_rx(), when it is about to complete (work_done < budget)
    and still owns the NAPI instance, checks stmmac_rx_dirty(). At
    STMMAC_RX_FILL_BATCH (16) or more buffers missing it arms the timer for
    STMMAC_RX_REFILL_RETRY_MS (50 ms). The RX interrupt is unmasked as before;
    whichever of timer and interrupt comes first schedules the poll.
  - __stmmac_disable_all_queues() deletes the timers after rx_napi is off, so
    no retry is pending while NAPI is disabled. stmmac_enable_all_queues()
    arms it at once for a queue still short of buffers: the tc offload path
    disables and enables the queues without rebuilding the rings, and its
    stall would otherwise outlive the call. Open rebuilds the rings and resume
    resets their indices, so there the check finds nothing to do. The XSK
    pool paths disable and enable rx_napi on their own and rebuild the ring;
    a retry still pending there fires once into the disabled rx_napi, which
    napi_schedule() ignores.
  - The zero-copy path is untouched. Without need_wakeup, stmmac_rx_zc()
    returns the budget when a refill fails, so NAPI polls on; with
    need_wakeup it returns count and sets the XSK need_wakeup flag, so user
    space kicks the queue (stmmac_main.c:5461-5470, 6.18.53). The note on
    v6 of 0bb05e6adfa9: that path is not affected by this stall.

Why the threshold catches every stall. stmmac_rx() leaves its loop for one of
three reasons (stmmac_main.c, 6.18.53): the budget is used up, the next
descriptor is still owned by the DMA, or the next entry is dirty_rx. After a
refill that got every page, stmmac_rx_dirty() is 0. The first exit means NAPI
polls again anyway. The second means the DMA still owns a descriptor, so the
next frame raises an interrupt and the refill is tried again. Only the third
leaves the DMA without a descriptor, and then stmmac_rx_dirty() is
dma_rx_size - 1, at least 63 (DMA_MIN_RX_SIZE is 64), well above 16.

Why 50 ms. msecs_to_jiffies(50) is 13 jiffies at our HZ=250, i.e. 52 ms --
the same as forcedeth's OOM_REFILL (1 + HZ/20, forcedeth.c:475), the in-tree
NIC that already retries an out-of-memory RX refill from a timer. virtio_net
waits HZ/2 from a workqueue (virtio_net.c:2975), bcm63xx_enet HZ from a timer
(bcm63xx_enet.c:277); both are slower. A shorter period buys nothing: while
the refill fails the ring stays empty whatever the period, and once memory is
back the queue restarts within one period. 10 ms would be 3 jiffies (12 ms)
here and four times the polls under OOM. The cost at 50 ms is 20 polls per
second per starved queue, each giving up at the first allocation that fails.
That is not the busy loop the review rejected.

What this patch does NOT fix. It ends the "for ever": the queue restarts
once the refill succeeds again. It does not stop PAUSE during the memory
shortage itself. While the refill fails the MTL RX queue stays full, and with
TX flow control on (EHFC set) the MAC keeps sending PAUSE
for as long as the shortage lasts. On 2026-09-27 MemAvailable stayed near 0
for about 6 minutes. Whether that part needs its own change is open; the
test boot measures how long such an episode lasts.

Known risk, same thread. Russell King tested v4 on a Jetson Xavier (dwmac
5.0, <ac6kfQ98Xjt3dCGj@shell.armlinux.org.uk>, 2026-04-02): the RX ring ran
dry under iperf3, and after the ring was refilled and the tail pointer
written the DMA did not restart -- RBU set, CURRDESAPTR equal to the tail,
40 packets waiting in the MTL queue. He adds that it happens without the
patches too (<ac6m2E6FIu4azGj0@shell.armlinux.org.uk>); dma_rmb() in the
descriptor status reads helped but did not cure it
(<adjrtRSepmac2hpN@shell.armlinux.org.uk>). Maxime Chevallier reports a
similar RX stall on dwmac1000 (socfpga) with plain iperf3 on net-next,
"not always reproducible, it may or may not show up when I run iperf3 after
a fresh restart" -- no memory pressure is mentioned
(<8f780d3f-c4ef-4976-ad0b-60718c29e06a@bootlin.com>). Sam Edwards' RK3588
(dwmac 4.20) resumes after the same condition. Our core is 5.30. If it
behaves like the Xavier, this patch refills the ring and the queue still
does not move: that is the falsifier below.

Options not taken:
  - Returning the budget while the ring is short (v4 2/2): the busy loop.
  - Treating RBU as an RX trigger: RBUE is not in the dwmac4 interrupt mask in
    6.18.53 (DMA_CHAN_INTR_DEFAULT_MASK_4_10). Mainline enables it since
    1b9707e6f1a9 (v7.1) for the counters only. For xgmac2 it was turned off
    again ("net: stmmac: xgmac2: disable RBUE in default RX interrupt mask",
    accepted 2026-06-09) because it gave an interrupt storm and "RBUE does not
    provide OOM recovery". An RBU interrupt reruns the same refill with no
    delay.
  - Forcing TX pause off on the interface: it protects the link partner, not
    the receive path. It stays the measurement's segment guard.

Diagnostics, visible by default until the hardware record exists:
  "RX queue N: D of S buffers missing, retrying the refill every 50 ms"
  (netdev_warn) and "RX queue N refilled after K retries" (netdev_info).
  The first is printed once per episode, only once the episode outlasts one
  retry (STMMAC_RX_REFILL_LOG_RETRIES), and only under net_ratelimit(); the
  second only for an episode whose first line was printed. So a ring that
  hovers around the threshold cannot flood a 115200 baud console, and the
  two lines always come in pairs. Their visibility goes down once the patch
  is proven; the lines stay.

Found on the 6 GiB board, image 6.18.53, 2026-09-27: end0
stopped receiving about 18 s after an OOM burst and did not recover after the
load ended. Its MAC sent 298 PAUSE frames in 10 s while the board was idle,
until ip link set end0 down/up.

Verified: applies at --fuzz=0 on the full series over 6.18.53, and
drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/ builds with W=1.
NOT verified on hardware.

Expected on silicon, segment guarded (tx pause forced off), with the refill
failure forced by a test-only patch (net-stmmac-inject-rx-refill-failures, not carried) or brought
on by memory pressure: the warn line while the refill fails, then "refilled
after K retries" once it succeeds again, end0 receiving again with no
down/up, and DMA_CH0_Status RBU clear with the current RX descriptor != the
RX tail pointer.
Falsifier: the stall persisting after the "refilled" line (the tail pointer
write does not resume the RX DMA on this core, as on the Xavier above: RBU
still set, current == tail); or no warn line while end0 has stopped
receiving and the ring was starved (then the stall is not this mechanism);
or retries going on with memory available (the refill fails for another
reason).

For a comparison run without this
patch: A7S_SKIP_PATCHES=net-stmmac-retry-a-failed-rx-refill-from-a-timer.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac.h
@@ -145,6 +145,9 @@
 	struct stmmac_priv *priv_data;
 	spinlock_t lock;
 	u32 index;
+	struct timer_list rx_refill_timer;
+	u32 rx_refill_retries;
+	bool rx_refill_logged;
 };
 
 struct stmmac_fpe_cfg {
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/stmmac_main.c
@@ -84,6 +84,12 @@
 #define STMMAC_TX_XSK_AVAIL		16
 #define STMMAC_RX_FILL_BATCH		16
 
+/* Delay before an RX refill that ran out of memory is tried again, and the
+ * retry from which on the episode is logged (one retry is routine)
+ */
+#define STMMAC_RX_REFILL_RETRY_MS	50
+#define STMMAC_RX_REFILL_LOG_RETRIES	2
+
 #define STMMAC_XDP_PASS		0
 #define STMMAC_XDP_CONSUMED	BIT(0)
 #define STMMAC_XDP_TX		BIT(1)
@@ -135,6 +141,7 @@
 static void stmmac_reset_rx_queue(struct stmmac_priv *priv, u32 queue);
 static void stmmac_reset_tx_queue(struct stmmac_priv *priv, u32 queue);
 static void stmmac_reset_queues_param(struct stmmac_priv *priv);
+static inline u32 stmmac_rx_dirty(struct stmmac_priv *priv, u32 queue);
 static void stmmac_tx_timer_arm(struct stmmac_priv *priv, u32 queue);
 static void stmmac_flush_tx_descriptors(struct stmmac_priv *priv, int queue);
 static void stmmac_set_dma_operation_mode(struct stmmac_priv *priv, u32 txmode,
@@ -218,6 +225,13 @@
 		if (queue < tx_queues_cnt)
 			napi_disable(&ch->tx_napi);
 	}
+
+	/* rx_napi is off; stmmac_enable_all_queues() re-arms a needed retry */
+	for (queue = 0; queue < rx_queues_cnt; queue++) {
+		timer_delete_sync(&priv->channel[queue].rx_refill_timer);
+		priv->channel[queue].rx_refill_retries = 0;
+		priv->channel[queue].rx_refill_logged = false;
+	}
 }
 
 /**
@@ -262,8 +276,15 @@
 			continue;
 		}
 
-		if (queue < rx_queues_cnt)
+		if (queue < rx_queues_cnt) {
+			/* A ring left short of buffers raises no RX interrupt */
+			bool starved = stmmac_rx_dirty(priv, queue) >=
+				       STMMAC_RX_FILL_BATCH;
+
 			napi_enable(&ch->rx_napi);
+			if (starved)
+				mod_timer(&ch->rx_refill_timer, jiffies);
+		}
 		if (queue < tx_queues_cnt)
 			napi_enable(&ch->tx_napi);
 	}
@@ -5785,6 +5806,57 @@
 	return count;
 }
 
+static void stmmac_rx_refill_timer(struct timer_list *t)
+{
+	struct stmmac_channel *ch = timer_container_of(ch, t, rx_refill_timer);
+
+	napi_schedule(&ch->rx_napi);
+}
+
+/**
+ * stmmac_rx_refill_retry - retry a failed RX refill after a delay
+ * @priv: driver private structure
+ * @ch: RX channel whose poll is about to complete
+ * Description: stmmac_rx_refill() stops at the first buffer it cannot
+ * allocate and is tried again only by the next RX poll. Once the DMA owns
+ * no descriptor it raises no RX interrupt, so that poll never comes and the
+ * queue stays stalled after memory is back. When the ring is short of
+ * STMMAC_RX_FILL_BATCH buffers or more, schedule the poll again after
+ * STMMAC_RX_REFILL_RETRY_MS instead of polling in a loop.
+ * An episode is logged once it outlasts one retry, and only under
+ * net_ratelimit(); its end is logged only if its start was.
+ * Must be called while the poll still owns the NAPI instance.
+ */
+static void stmmac_rx_refill_retry(struct stmmac_priv *priv,
+				   struct stmmac_channel *ch)
+{
+	u32 dirty = stmmac_rx_dirty(priv, ch->index);
+
+	if (likely(dirty < STMMAC_RX_FILL_BATCH)) {
+		if (unlikely(ch->rx_refill_retries)) {
+			if (ch->rx_refill_logged)
+				netdev_info(priv->dev,
+					    "RX queue %u refilled after %u retries\n",
+					    ch->index, ch->rx_refill_retries);
+			ch->rx_refill_retries = 0;
+			ch->rx_refill_logged = false;
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (++ch->rx_refill_retries == STMMAC_RX_REFILL_LOG_RETRIES &&
+	    net_ratelimit()) {
+		netdev_warn(priv->dev,
+			    "RX queue %u: %u of %u buffers missing, retrying the refill every %u ms\n",
+			    ch->index, dirty, priv->dma_conf.dma_rx_size,
+			    STMMAC_RX_REFILL_RETRY_MS);
+		ch->rx_refill_logged = true;
+	}
+
+	mod_timer(&ch->rx_refill_timer,
+		  jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(STMMAC_RX_REFILL_RETRY_MS));
+}
+
 static int stmmac_napi_poll_rx(struct napi_struct *napi, int budget)
 {
 	struct stmmac_channel *ch =
@@ -5800,6 +5872,8 @@
 	u64_stats_update_end(&rxq_stats->napi_syncp);
 
 	work_done = stmmac_rx(priv, budget, chan);
+	if (work_done < budget)
+		stmmac_rx_refill_retry(priv, ch);
 	if (work_done < budget && napi_complete_done(napi, work_done)) {
 		unsigned long flags;
 
@@ -7391,6 +7465,8 @@
 
 		if (queue < priv->plat->rx_queues_to_use) {
 			netif_napi_add(dev, &ch->rx_napi, stmmac_napi_poll_rx);
+			timer_setup(&ch->rx_refill_timer,
+				    stmmac_rx_refill_timer, 0);
 		}
 		if (queue < priv->plat->tx_queues_to_use) {
 			netif_napi_add_tx(dev, &ch->tx_napi,
